s":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":38,"department":"at-least-there-was-some-jack-black","hitParade":[38,36,29,21,6,4,1],"section":"games","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Spike TV Video Game Award Winners","uri":"http://games.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/224214&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"drizzle writes \\"There\'s an interesting story on the Apache Marketing blog about whether or not Apache projects come with too much overhead, especially compared with other services or a roll-your-own approach. The article states, \'It\'s true that compared with SourceForge, Apache has a more rigorous management structure. The ASF has formalized processes and procedures that we believe represent best practices governance. All new projects must pass through an incubation period to ensure that all of the project\'s members have internalized these processes. However, each project\'s leadership has a tremendous amount of discretion in managing within this framework.\' There is also a follow up article written by one of the httpd developers about \'What Apache brings to the table.\' The article cites community, experience, legal framework, diversity, brand strength, and networking as reasons why developers and companies should consider bringing their projects over to Apache.\\"<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1959\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://apache.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/2010223&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1959","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["Zonk"],"dates":["2005-11-19T22:39:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"software"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":62,"department":"many-hands-make-light-work","hitParade":[62,60,40,30,18,12,8],"section":"apache","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Apache Comes With Too Much Community Overhead?","uri":"http://apache.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/2010223&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"mykdavies writes \\"The Guardian has an excellent article giving lay readers an overview of some of the problems being caused by the concept of \'intellectual property\', including references to stories familiar to Slashdot readers, such as DVD Jon, the Sony rootkit, Amazon and Google business patents.\\" From the article: \\"Even facts about the world can, in some cases, become the property of commercial companies. It was the promise of gaining patents on the human genome that lured investors into the private consortium that attempted to sequence it in competition with the public effort. Laboratory animals have already been patented, starting with the OncoMouse, an animal whose genome has been manipulated to ensure that it develops cancer.\\"<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1958\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://yro.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/208219&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1958","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["Zonk"],"dates":["2005-11-19T21:27:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"patents"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":110,"department":"ins-and-outs","hitParade":[110,105,76,45,20,7,2],"section":"yro","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"The Guardian On Intellectual Property","uri":"http://yro.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/208219&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"nevermindme writes \\"Cisco has agreed to plunk down a whopping $6.9bn for Scientific-Atlanta, hoping to take a major share of the set-top-box market and push video efforts. The networking giant will pay $43 per share in cash for Scientific-Atlanta ($5.3bn) and swallow $1.6bn in debt. With the buy, Cisco acquires one of the more dominant set-top-box makers. In addition, it pushes well beyond the data center to touch consumers where they live.\\"\\n<p><a href=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/~c/Slashdot/slashdot?a=UAEMGp\\"><img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/~c/Slashdot/slashdot?i=UAEMGp\\" border=\\"0\\"></img></a></p><img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1957\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://hardware.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/1810238&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1957","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["Zonk"],"dates":["2005-11-19T20:30:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"tv"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":72,"department":"new-horizons","hitParade":[72,68,51,35,16,9,5],"section":"hardware","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Cisco Moving On Set-Top Boxes","uri":"http://hardware.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/1810238&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"Carl Bialik from the WSJ writes \\"Microsoft is hoping its Xbox 360 will further the company\'s goal to \'link the Web and entertainment of all forms in consumers\' living rooms,\' the Wall Street Journal reports, but \'one manufacturing misstep -- a shortage of graphics chips or a recalled hard drive -- could derail those ambitions and drag Microsoft\'s unprofitable videogame business even deeper into the red.\' The WSJ traces the 1,700 parts that go into the device through the supply chain -- from two southern China factories, Rotterdam, and on to Toledo, Memphis, and ultimately, retailers in the U.S. -- and looks at what could go wrong along the way.\\"<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1956\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://games.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/18/156253&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1956","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["Zonk"],"dates":["2005-11-19T19:36:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"xbox"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":141,"department":"complicated-process","hitParade":[141,140,103,73,19,12,8],"section":"games","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Getting All 1,700 Parts of the Xbox 360 to Market","uri":"http://games.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/18/156253&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"theodp writes \\"Over at Slate, Avi Zenilman has seen the real classroom of the future firsthand: Students use class time to read the Drudge Report, send e-mail, play Legend of Zelda, or update profiles on Facebook.com. But not to worry - replace laptops with crumpled notes, and the classroom of the future looks a lot like the classroom of the past.\\" From the article: \\"... when Cornell University researchers outfitted classrooms with wireless Internet and monitored students\' browsing habits, they concluded, \'Longer browsing sessions during class tend to lead to lower grades, but there\'s a hint that a greater number of browsing sessions during class may actually lead to higher grades.\' It seems a bit of a stretch to impute a causal relationship, but it\'s certainly possible that the kind of brain that can handle multiple channels of information is also the kind of brain that earns A\'s.\\"<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1955\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://hardware.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/186252&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1955","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["Zonk"],"dates":["2005-11-19T18:46:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"ed"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":279,"department":"no","hitParade":[279,272,210,140,43,23,13],"section":"hardware","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Is Wi-Fi Ruining College?","uri":"http://hardware.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/186252&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"john writes \\"Sony Pictures Home Entertainment announced that authoring has been completed on the first Blu-ray Disc (BD) to contain a full-length, high-definition feature film. Charlie\'s Angels: Full Throttle was compressed and authored in MPEG 2 full high-definition (1920 x 1080) and is now being shipped to BD hardware companies for player testing.\\"\\n<p><a href=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/~c/Slashdot/slashdot?a=OZPTC3\\"><img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/~c/Slashdot/slashdot?i=OZPTC3\\" border=\\"0\\"></img></a></p><img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1954\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/1534223&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1954","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["CowboyNeal"],"dates":["2005-11-19T17:13:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"sony"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":210,"department":"hot-off-the-presses","hitParade":[210,200,156,106,32,21,13],"section":"mainpage","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Sony Completes First Full-Length Blu-ray Disc","uri":"http://slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/1534223&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"ndansmith writes \\"Bruce Perens posts in his blog about an amusing encounter between Richard Stallman and United Nations security at the World Summit on the Information Society in Tunis. It seems that RFID technology, which Stallman opposes for privacy reasons, was used in the identification badges for the conference. From the blog: \'You can\'t give Richard a visible RF ID strip without expecting him to protest. Richard acquired an entire roll of aluminum foil and wore his foil-shielded pass prominently.\' During a keynote speech, Stallman also passed around the tinfoil for other to use as well. It seems that UN security was not amused, however, as they would not let him leave the room for some time.\\" What makes this even funnier, of course, is that tin foil hats won\'t stop them.<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1953\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/149242&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1953","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["CowboyNeal"],"dates":["2005-11-19T16:04:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"humor"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":425,"department":"of-all-the-reasons","hitParade":[425,412,333,250,97,57,42],"section":"mainpage","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Richard Stallman Accosted For Tinfoil Hat","uri":"http://slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/149242&from=rss"},{"author":null,"categories":[],"comments":null,"content":null,"description":{"type":"text/plain","value":"Momoru writes \\"The Baltimore Sun is reporting that a Canadian company, Delcan NET, will begin testing a technology that determines the flow of automobile traffic by monitoring cell phone traffic. The company promises a revolutionary way to determine backups, but privacy advocates fear the implications of a third party tracking users by their cell phones.\\"<img src=\\"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?g=1952\\"/>"},"enclosures":[],"expirationDate":null,"foreignMarkup":["<feedburner:origLink xmlns:feedburner=\\"http://rssnamespace.org/feedburner/ext/1.0\\">http://yro.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/143247&amp;from=rss</feedburner:origLink>"],"guid":null,"link":"http://rss.slashdot.org/Slashdot/slashdot?m=1952","modules":[{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["CowboyNeal"],"dates":["2005-11-19T15:10:00.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":[],"publishers":[],"relations":[],"rightsList":[],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"privacy"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"},{"comments":118,"department":"big-brother-is-listening","hitParade":[118,114,75,49,23,20,14],"section":"yro","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/slash/"}],"pubDate":null,"source":null,"title":"Baltimore to Test Cell Phone Traffic Monitoring","uri":"http://yro.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=05/11/19/143247&from=rss"}],"language":null,"lastBuildDate":null,"link":"http://slashdot.org/","managingEditor":null,"modules":[{"updateBase":"1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z","updateFrequency":1,"updatePeriod":"hourly","uri":"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/syndication/"},{"contributors":[],"coverages":[],"creators":["pater@slashdot.org"],"dates":["2005-11-20T01:11:49.000Z"],"descriptions":[],"formats":[],"identifiers":[],"languages":["en-us"],"publishers":["OSTG"],"relations":[],"rightsList":["Copyright 1997-2005, OSTG - Open Source Technology Group, Inc.  All Rights Reserved."],"sources":[],"subjects":[{"taxonomyUri":null,"value":"Technology"}],"titles":[],"types":[],"uri":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"}],"pubDate":null,"rating":null,"skipDays":[],"skipHours":[],"styleSheet":null,"textInput":{"description":"Search Slashdot stories","link":"http://slashdot.org/search.pl","name":"query","title":"Search Slashdot"},"title":"Slashdot","ttl":-1,"uri":"http://slashdot.org/","webMaster":null}'
